Z||000012||KissMySSFiero||05-09-2001||11:28 PM||||IMO...people on here can be just as narrow minded as people on the other forums.  flame on.  personally i would never drive a CRX.  but one of the guys i used to work with had one.  and we would talk about cars all day at work.  beat actaully workin.  =Þ   but to me when some guy drops a honda prelude b1600 or whatever they call the engines,  into his little honda crx.  to me thats the same as me puttin a SC 3800 in my fiero.  its all about makin your car perform better and go faster.  car people all have the samething in mind.  go fast.  well most of them anyways.  i like anyone who wants to make their car or any method of transportation,  faster.  (ive seen snowmobiles run 10's in the 1/4)  but when one guy says his car is better simply because its a honda or chevy or fiero,  thats when it just pisses me off.  CANT WE ALL JUST GET ALONG.  <BR>personally ive driven an rx7.  my brother had one.  not a bad car.  his was an 87.  just plain jane rotory.  they both were about equal when it came to straight line racing.  but i lke the way my fiero handles by far.  however the braking IMO was far better in the rx7.  i dont know about stopping distance,  but i could feel if  a piece of sand was on the rotors.  mine are not at all lke that.  anyways.  too much already.  I like my fiero because its different.  thats just the way its gotta be.  =Þ<BR><P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>      <P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">          <BR>"When in Doubt..Whip it out!!"||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroMaster88||05-10-2001||12:07 AM||||They have their own FORUM???????? <P>HA ha ha ha ha haha haha  aaaahahahahahahahaha ahahahah ha ha ha....<BR>Thats funny!<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">        <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/smiling2.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000014||Black88GT||05-10-2001||12:16 AM||||Yeah RX7s are pretty popular.  I have heard a million people say the same thing about the Fiero forum ironically enough...||||reg||1
Z||000015||WKDFIRO||05-10-2001||12:17 AM||||I love RX-7s. I was really bummed when I sat in the last generation version and had my head feeling like it was going out the sunroof. I love the RX-7 owners that start bad mouthing Fieros. At that point I nod my head and admit that yeah, RX-7s dominated IMSA racing for a long time....Until 1987.<P>In 1987, Pontiac captured the IMSA Manufacturer’s Cup in GTP-Lights marking the first time anyone had been able to defeat Mazda. In 16 racing events, FIERO GTPs accounted for 11 of the fastest qualifying positions, 10 fastest race laps and won 9 times. Using the Super Duty 4 engine.<P>I usually have a big smile by that time...<BR> <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||Ed||05-10-2001||12:35 AM||||Some of the responses were okay.<P>I've had first hand experience driving "against" an RX7.  The one "sports car" I passed relatively easily in SCCA's  Thunderhill Park (racetrack) performance driving school was a first generation RX7.  <P>I'd think overall the first generation RX7 and V-6 Fiero are pretty equal as someone on the other board pointed out.  As mentioned on another topic (dropped throttle oversteer) the Road and Track of August 1985 showed the GT Fiero to be superior in many categories and rated superior overall to the "small engine" RX7.<P>I looked at both in 1984 and bought an SE then (I have a V-6 GT now).  Remember, the four cylinder Fiero at that time was about $2,000 or $3,000 cheaper than the "small engine" RX7.  The V-6 in 1985 was about $2,000 cheaper, a considerable sum almost 20 years ago.  The "big engine" RX7 GSL-SE was quite a bit more, but it did also include some luxury items.  <P>If someone with an RX7 starts to give you a hard time about Fieros, just say "apex seals" and "gas milage."<P>EVERY car has its weak points.  I was reading about Ferrari 348's, a $60,000 to $80,000 car in the used car market.  There were ALL KINDS of things to watch out for.<P>To each his own.<P> <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||05-10-2001||12:53 AM||||I'd take a 1993 RX7 turbo over a Fiero.  I drove one, it was an amazing car.<P>On the other hand, I can buy 15 2M4s, a half dozen Fiero GTs or build at least two beautiful Fiero V8s for the cost of a decent 1993 RX7 turbo.  <P>The older Rx7s are interesting cars, but spark plugs are like $30 to $50 each.  I guess if I had one, I would drive it.  The same goes for a Porsche 924.  A Porsche 928 would be a f***ing blast!  <P>There's lots of cool cars.  But, a 5 spd Indy Fiero with a 16 valve Super Duty engine is what I would LOVE to have!  <P>Does anyone have a car like that?  What's it like?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Greg Piet||05-10-2001||12:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>The older Rx7s are interesting cars, but spark plugs are like $30 to $50 each.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uhhh  I dont know what first gen rex's you have worked on and where you buy your spark plugs, but normal old spark plugs work fine in the 12a and 13b motors.||||reg||1
Z||000019||JohnnyK||05-10-2001||01:10 AM||||F-Body guys are the WORST... THey are ignorant and annoying. I love the rx-7.. A great car!||||reg||1
Z||000020||SWTKSTMS||05-10-2001||11:12 AM||||i have drove some of the rx7s and they are fun to drive. but to compare i would much rather take a fiero because of the whole mid engine. it seems to be nice to have that engine on top of the back wheels. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Spektyr||05-10-2001||11:52 AM||||Well I'll share a little story with you about how I came to be a Fiero owner.<P>A lady where I work was trying to sell her 1967 all original straight-6 Mustang.  It was pretty rough and needed quite a bit of work, but it seemed like a good deal for the 1000 cash she was asking.  The next day a good friend of mine asked me if I'd be interested in taking his Fiero off his hands as he was tired of paying for repairs (he's not a wrench-turner).  I told him I probably wouldn't be interested as the 'Stang was going to eat up a lot of my time.<P>Anyway, after some investigation on the 'Stang, I found out that this particular specimen had SERIOUS cowl cancer that had spread to the floorboards.  As I was not interested in drilling out 125 spot welds to crack open the cowl and then butt-weld in some steel to rebuild it, and THEN replace the floor pan (Which would run at least 4k to pay someone else to do) I passed on the 'Stang and told my buddy I'd take the Fiero.<P>My original intention was to get the Fiero, fix the shifter problem with it, and then resell it for some money to buy my 'real' project car.  (I didn't think I'd like the Fiero, and figured it was probably too small for me to drive at 6'4").  Well, it drove amazingly well on the way home considering my gear choices were limited to 2nd and 4th gear.  The Fiero fit me quite comfortably.  I found this forum to get advice on repairing it, found the missing C-clamp on the transaxle cable, replaced it and fell in love.<P>The only thing missing was my old-fashioned muscle car rumble, gigantic body twisting torque, and generally all the stuff that sends Tim 'The Tool Man' Taylor on a grunting spree.<P>Then I followed a link to V8 Archie's site.  Then I learned about the 3.X turbos... and the 3800SC.  Then I was in real trouble.  I realized I owned a car capable of all the handling of a modern sports car and all the old-fashioned grunt of a musclecar.<P>'67 Mustang?  What's that?  Oh yeah, a car I used to want.<P>So let them drive they're big metal musclecars, their little plastic/aluminum ricer cars, or their rotary powered sports cars.  Sure, there'll be some that beat some Fieros.  Heck, a lot of stock cars beat my 85GT right now.  The Fiero is never going to win "Fastest street legal car in America", or probably any other "Fastest" category.  No matter what you drive the odds are someone has a car that beats yours in speed, handling, reliability, braking, stereo, power window speed, electric lighter warm-up, etc.  And someone is always going to laugh at you because their car is more multicolored, newer, lower, or some other measurement that is important to <I>them</I> but not <I>you</I>.<P>Me, I just love my cars.  I even love my Town Car that can only beat about half the economy cars built at a street light, and gets about 1/3 the mileage.  I love getting in my Fiero and hearing the no-cat exhaust rumble coming from BEHIND me instead of in front.  Yeah, I laugh at the ricer-boys who think the Folger's Coffee can welded to their exhaust tip sounds cool.  Of course they're laughing at me because my car doesn't set off nearby car alarms with the exhaust OR the 4million watt stereo system.  Will they beat me off the line?  Good chance.  Once I finally decide what motor I want and get it installed?  Maybe.<P>Will it change their mind and make them buy a Fiero if I win?  Nope, they'll bolt more stuff to their rice-mobile.<P>Will I still love my Fiero if I lose?  You bet.<P>The Fiero is an incredibly unique car.  Space-frame, mid-engine, multiple engine swaps, multiple body kits, fantastic handling, beautiful styling.  <I>We're</I> the ones using the strange 'measuring stick' on our automobiles.  There simply aren't any American cars to compare it to.  So we draw a lot of flak <I>because</I> we're different, <I>because</I> we measure our cars differently.<P>They aren't right, we aren't right.  We're just different.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000022||rogergarrison||05-10-2001||12:59 PM||||I just let everyone drive what they want, makes me no difference. Our car club has all european made cars...or replicas. we have from MGs to Carrera Turbos, and Panteras. We all get along great. Im also good friends with a bunch of guys in one of the biggest Corvette clubs. I even run with them on road trips, we have a lot of good humored ribbing going both ways. Good people are good people no matter what they drive.||||reg||1
Z||000023||84Bill||05-10-2001||01:04 PM||||How long have their cars been out of production?||||reg||5
Z||000024||Voytek||05-10-2001||01:09 PM||||..Sniff, ..sniff, (blow, blow).<BR>That was touching Spektyr.<P>Here's another short story.  I went with Flashy to a store a couple of weeks ago.  We parked our two '87 GT's in front (yes, we had to drive both of them).  When we came out, there was a lady with her daughter looking inside of our cars and checking out the exterior.  When we got to our cars, she asked what they were and said they look awesome.  After leaving the groceries in the cars, we walked to a liquor store (same parking lot).  After coming out, we've noticed the strangest sight:  A GUY PARKED HIS '94 RX7 IN LINE WITH OUR CARS AND WAS TAKING PICTURES OF ALL 3 CARS TOGETHER.  He was a middle aged gentleman and exclaimed that the cars look great.  He took about 4 pictures from different angles.  This is my experience with a VERY NICE RX7 owner.<BR>While I love Fieros, the last generation RX7 looks awesome! (no, I wouldn't trade, but I would add one of those to my collection if I had $30,000 kicking around).  But then again, I'd probably buy a couple more Fieros first and soup them up.||||reg||7
Z||000025||mshill||05-10-2001||01:40 PM||||The RX7/Fiero stuff has come up here before.  I used to have an 82 RX7 (I think it had the 12A motor).  It was a fun car, but nowhere near as fun as the V6 Fiero.<P>And my brother-in-law (the doctor) has his third gen RX7 (I think it is a twin turbo, somewhere around 275hp) up for sale for about $27K.  That car would kick the stuffing out of just about any Fiero in the straights or curves.  However, in my Fiero I have really good speed and handling at one-tenth of the cost.  That's what's so great about the Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Ken Wittlief||05-10-2001||02:15 PM||||"When we came out, there was a lady with her daughter looking inside of our cars and checking out the exterior. When we got to our cars, she asked what they were and said they look awesome"<P>Im one of those people that likes to take the tags and labels off of everything, and plan on stipping all the identification I can off my fiero, so if you dont know what it is, you wont know what it is.<P> Im thinking of casting a front hood emblem out of silver to repace the Fiero one, not sure yet what it will look like.<P>the 'Fiero 2M6' will come off the engine deck when I paint it.<P>  cant do anything about the 'Pontaic' stamped into the alum wheels, but at least you dont see them when the wheels are spinning.<P>  the only one I dont know what to do with is the 'Pontiac' molded into the rear bumper. Maybe a bumper from another year doesnt have that pressed in there?||||reg||1
Z||000027||Greg Piet||05-10-2001||02:31 PM||||Well in case you didnt know I am Firebomb...<P>I was rather impressed with the caliber of discussion over there...  right up untill Mr "handles like a pig" Boy gets all defensive and personal.<P>I figured it would be a flame war from post one... <P>maybe we will get some rex'ers over here like the mr2 people were for a while||||reg||1
Z||000028||sqoach||05-10-2001||02:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR><B>Well I'll share a little story with you about how I came to be a Fiero owner.<P>A lady where I work was trying to sell her 1967 all original straight-6 Mustang.  It was pretty rough and needed quite a bit of work, but it seemed like a good deal for the 1000 cash she was asking.  The next day a good friend of mine asked me if I'd be interested in taking his Fiero off his hands as he was tired of paying for repairs (he's not a wrench-turner).  I told him I probably wouldn't be interested as the 'Stang was going to eat up a lot of my time.<P>Anyway, after some investigation on the 'Stang, I found out that this particular specimen had SERIOUS cowl cancer that had spread to the floorboards.  As I was not interested in drilling out 125 spot welds to crack open the cowl and then butt-weld in some steel to rebuild it, and THEN replace the floor pan (Which would run at least 4k to pay someone else to do) I passed on the 'Stang and told my buddy I'd take the Fiero.<P>My original intention was to get the Fiero, fix the shifter problem with it, and then resell it for some money to buy my 'real' project car.  (I didn't think I'd like the Fiero, and figured it was probably too small for me to drive at 6'4").  Well, it drove amazingly well on the way home considering my gear choices were limited to 2nd and 4th gear.  The Fiero fit me quite comfortably.  I found this forum to get advice on repairing it, found the missing C-clamp on the transaxle cable, replaced it and fell in love.<P>The only thing missing was my old-fashioned muscle car rumble, gigantic body twisting torque, and generally all the stuff that sends Tim 'The Tool Man' Taylor on a grunting spree.<P>Then I followed a link to V8 Archie's site.  Then I learned about the 3.X turbos... and the 3800SC.  Then I was in real trouble.  I realized I owned a car capable of all the handling of a modern sports car and all the old-fashioned grunt of a musclecar.<P>'67 Mustang?  What's that?  Oh yeah, a car I used to want.<P>So let them drive they're big metal musclecars, their little plastic/aluminum ricer cars, or their rotary powered sports cars.  Sure, there'll be some that beat some Fieros.  Heck, a lot of stock cars beat my 85GT right now.  The Fiero is never going to win "Fastest street legal car in America", or probably any other "Fastest" category.  No matter what you drive the odds are someone has a car that beats yours in speed, handling, reliability, braking, stereo, power window speed, electric lighter warm-up, etc.  And someone is always going to laugh at you because their car is more multicolored, newer, lower, or some other measurement that is important to <I>them</I> but not <I>you</I>.<P>Me, I just love my cars.  I even love my Town Car that can only beat about half the economy cars built at a street light, and gets about 1/3 the mileage.  I love getting in my Fiero and hearing the no-cat exhaust rumble coming from BEHIND me instead of in front.  Yeah, I laugh at the ricer-boys who think the Folger's Coffee can welded to their exhaust tip sounds cool.  Of course they're laughing at me because my car doesn't set off nearby car alarms with the exhaust OR the 4million watt stereo system.  Will they beat me off the line?  Good chance.  Once I finally decide what motor I want and get it installed?  Maybe.<P>Will it change their mind and make them buy a Fiero if I win?  Nope, they'll bolt more stuff to their rice-mobile.<P>Will I still love my Fiero if I lose?  You bet.<P>The Fiero is an incredibly unique car.  Space-frame, mid-engine, multiple engine swaps, multiple body kits, fantastic handling, beautiful styling.  <I>We're</I> the ones using the strange 'measuring stick' on our automobiles.  There simply aren't any American cars to compare it to.  So we draw a lot of flak <I>because</I> we're different, <I>because</I> we measure our cars differently.<P>They aren't right, we aren't right.  We're just different.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know what to say...it's just so...beautiful *sniff*||||reg||14
Z||000029||87ECO5||05-10-2001||02:46 PM||||Since it seems others are "coming out of the closet" too, I'll just hop on the bandwagon.<P>I love RX-7s.  Had two of them - an '84 GS and an '87 SE.  Both were WONDERFUL cars!  Nothing like revving a rotary to 8000 RPMs and having it be as smooth as my V6 at idle.  Both were good-looking (IMHO) and they were fun to drive.  What else is there?<P>Heck, I love MR2s, 300ZXs, and Supras too.  I'm a car guy, folks.  I love all cars!  Every car - INCLUDING the Fiero - has its pros and cons.  Get over it and get back to what's important - enjoying whatever it is you're driving!<P>This senseless - and regular - dosage of car bashing we get around here is purely a waste of bandwidth ... just like this post!||||reg||6
Z||000030||Butter||05-10-2001||03:33 PM||||Hey Yall,<BR>That feller what said "handles like a pig" ain't never tried to catcha greased pig before now has he. :rolleyes<p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Spektyr||05-11-2001||07:33 AM||||LOL<P>Lemme guess.  Your hometown would exactly be classified as 'urban', eh Butter?<P>(no offense, I'm a Midwest boy myself.  There aren't any real cities in the Midwest.  And the north part doesn't count, they aren't really in the Midwest anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )||||reg||1
Z||000032||Fiero5||05-11-2001||12:41 PM||||Go check the thread over there now.<BR>I had a little fun  Heh, heh, heh<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroSSsignature1_1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000033||Acuspeed||05-11-2001||05:04 PM||||I don't know, I felt the thread on the 7 forum was pretty good myself.  I believe the member who refered to the car handling "like a pig" was refering the the example his brother bought, not Fieros in general.  Lets face it, as cars get older things start to losen up -- the bushings may have been shot, the shocks/struts in need of replacement, the springs sagging, who knows.<P>I know when I bought my 79 '7, it handled scary.  It was twitchy and jittery.  But all it needed were some of the things I mentioned above, and it was much, much better.<P>The other thing to keep in mind is that this is in a generation specific section of our forum, in which the members are more "family like" and we kinda have gotten to know each other and each other's 'posting style' so to speak.  So, I can understand how, to a new member, the comments may have been taken a bit differently.<P>At any rate, to those of you on the RX-7 forum now, welcome.  <P>------------------<BR>Ken<BR>--Official Member of the Rotary Cult--<P>1979 RX-7 S<BR>1996 Probe GT<p>[This message has been edited by Acuspeed (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||JoshP||05-11-2001||06:03 PM||||I was the individual who started the post on the RX-7 board. <BR>I posted what i posted so i could learn more about the fiero and see what people thought.<BR>I commented on what my uncle said as a retort to what he said, not to the fiero.<BR>that would be awesome to have a FGT. i would like to have one. the individuals who also posted on the thread acted very civil and open-minded IMHO, (well some of them did!).<BR>i now know a whole lot more than i did when i first posted! that's why i posted! <BR>I do not neccessarily appreciate the flak that we got from you guys just for giving our opinions about your car. it wasn't meant to spite you guys, we didn't direct it at you, we just shared opinions.<BR>Josh||||reg||14
Z||000035||Felix Wankel||05-12-2001||02:41 AM||||Just to clarify, I never bashed the Fiero. <P>I was just poking fun at firebomb's choice of usernames. I know that the fires were a limited problem to the early models. <P>I researched the Fiero quite heavily inthe couple weeks I had one (86 2M4). I had intended on building a 400 SBC for it. I wound up selling it instead.<P>FWIW, a stock 1st gen runs ~high 16's in the 1/4. Its very well possibe that you were beating them with your 4 cylinder ones. But you better bring a 2M6 to race mine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I've actually had quite a good time racing against a few 2M6's in my old 88 RX-7. Close race. Just the way I like it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have to agree with what was posted above about being an enthusiast. A true enthusiast can appreciate <I>all</I> cars, they just choose to own the one that appeals to them. You guys want Fieros, we want RX-7's. Simple as that. Why can't we each respect what the other is capable of? I know I do.<P>Again, none of this is meant to be inflammatory in any way. Just stating my opinion.<P>(BTW, I still want a Fiero GT with a 3.8 GN engine. 700 hp in that car would be much fun!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.rx7club.com/ubb/image_uploads/fbrear1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
